The Track B audit also fails. I tested the strongest plausible escape hatch—an inverse-generated non-subgroup factorization built from mixed-radix digit factors, which is more general than the paper’s displayed subgroup code. At a representative \(n=16{,}384\), \(|S|=1{,}024\), \(|C|=16\), cyclic exact-cover search completed in 17 nodes on every tested seed (roughly \(6\times10^{-5}\) to \(6\times10^{-3}\) seconds after parsing). The compact digit-mask route is about 35 operations, so both routes are tiny and qualitatively the same decomposition; there is no defensible Track B compression gap. I’m recording a Step-0 rejection rather than building a generator around a known-easy distribution.
